Acknowledgements
================================================================================

XDRPeak builds on the work of several open-source projects and developers
who pioneered the techniques used to push Apple displays beyond 500 nits.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BrightXDR — Dmitry Starkov (starkdmi)
https://github.com/starkdmi/BrightXDR
Licensed under GPL-3.0.

The original BrightXDR demonstrated the menu-bar utility model for unlocking
Apple's eXtended Dynamic Range mode programmatically. XDRPeak inherits the
GPL-3.0 license from this upstream project.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
BrightIntosh — Niklas Rousset (niklasr22)
https://github.com/niklasr22/BrightIntosh
Licensed under GPL-3.0.

The 1×1 HDR pixel technique used to flip the display into EDR mode without
a full-screen overlay, the per-chip gamma factor values (1.59 on M1/M2,
1.535 on M3+), and the EDR clear-color formula
(maxEDR / max(maxRenderedEDR, 1.0) - 1.0) all come from BrightIntosh.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Lunar — Alin Panaitiu (alin23)
https://lunar.fyi · https://github.com/alin23/Lunar
Licensed under GPL-3.0.

Lunar's analysis of EDR headroom signals on Apple displays and its Full
Range mode investigation informed XDRPeak's pre-flight headroom poll, which
prevents applying gamma > 1.0 before EDR mode has actually engaged on the
display (avoiding the "all-white screen" failure mode).

